\subsection{Scenario C: High Juvenile Mortality (Bearded Vulture Baseline)}
Informed by empirical field data on large raptors \cite{bustamante1996}, first-year mortality is elevated to $d_1 = 0.659$ ($65.9\%$), while immature and adult mortalities remain at $d_I = d_A = 0.086$.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{Fig_c_1987.png}
    \hfill
    \includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{Fig_c_2022.png}
    \caption{50-year projection under high first-year mortality ($d_1 = 0.659, d_I = d_A = 0.086$). Left: Initial Conditions of 22 Adult condors. Right: Initial conditions of the 2022 condor population.}
    \label{fig:system_and_field}
\end{figure}

The extremely high mortality rate for the first year of life causes a significantly lower growth rate as the majority of birds fail to make it to their second year of life. This also reduces the number of birds that make it to adulthood and can reproduce. The growth rate cannot keep up with the high death rate, and so the population declines. 

\subsection{Scenario D: High Juvenile, Moderate Immature, and Low Adult Mortality}
This scenario pairs high first-year mortality ($d_1 = 0.659$) with moderate immature losses ($d_I = 0.138$) and low adult mortality ($d_A = 0.069$).

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{Fig_d_1987.png}
    \hfill
